entry radiation
term: Radiation
image: ../../assets/images/terms/radiation.webp
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
related_terms: background-radiation
related_terms: decay
related_terms: x-ray
quick_facts: Two families: waves (EM) and particles
quick_facts: Ionizing vs non-ionizing is what matters for health
quick_facts: Bananas are mildly radioactive from potassium-40
quick_facts: Sunlight is radiation — so is Wi-Fi, at very different energies
quick_facts: Background radiation predates humanity by eons

sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"What is Radiation?","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/radiation"}

definition:
    Energy traveling outward from its source — either as electromagnetic waves (radio, infrared, light, UV, X-rays, gamma) or fast particles (alpha, beta, neutrons). The dividing line that matters biologically is energy per quantum: ionizing radiation can tear electrons off atoms and break DNA; non-ionizing radiation mostly just heats things. Radiation is everywhere and always has been — sunlight, rocks, bananas, outer space.

debunking_snippet:
    “Any amount of radiation is unsafe.” Life evolved amid constant natural radiation — every second, thousands of natural particles pass through you harmlessly. Risk depends on dose, and small doses carry vanishingly small risk.
